<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sirmoor Rifles</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles (The Sirmoor Rifles) was a Gurkha regiment of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>, raised in 1815 at Nahan in Sirmur as the Sirmoor Battalion from soldiers who had served the Gurkha army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a>. It won fame defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Rao's house at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1857. In 1947 it passed to the British Army, and in 1994 it was merged into the Royal Gurkha Rifles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2nd-king-edward-viis-own-gurkha-rifles.jpg" alt="2nd King Edward VII's Own Gurkha Rifles" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Crowthrower · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sirmoor2GR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
